/**
@page api_me_rap Matrix Exponential and Rational Arrival Process Distributions
@brief ME and RAP distribution classes for advanced queueing models

These modules provide support for Matrix Exponential (ME) and Rational Arrival Process (RAP)
distributions, which generalize phase-type and MAP distributions respectively.

@section me_overview Matrix Exponential (ME) Distribution

The Matrix Exponential distribution is a generalization of the phase-type (PH) distribution.
It is parameterized by an initial vector \f$\alpha\f$ and a matrix \f$A\f$, where \f$\alpha\f$
need not sum to 1 (unlike PH distributions).

<b>Mathematical Definition:</b>

The CDF is defined as:
\f[
F(t) = 1 - \alpha e^{At} \mathbf{e}
\f]

where \f$\mathbf{e}\f$ is a column vector of ones.

<b>Properties:</b>
- The initial vector \f$\alpha\f$ can have entries that sum to less than or greater than 1
- All eigenvalues of \f$A\f$ must have negative real parts
- The dominant eigenvalue (closest to zero) must be real
- More general than PH distributions (every PH is an ME, but not vice versa)

<b>Process Representation:</b>

Internally, ME distributions are represented as a two-matrix process:
- \f$D_0 = A\f$
- \f$D_1 = -A \mathbf{e} \alpha\f$

This allows compatibility with MAP-based algorithms for moment computation and analysis.

@subsection me_constructors Constructors

@subsubsection me_constructor_basic Basic Constructor

<b>MATLAB:</b>
@code
me = ME(alpha, A)
@endcode

<b>Java:</b>
@code
ME me = new ME(alpha, A);
@endcode

<b>Python:</b>
@code
me = ME(alpha, A)  # alpha and A can be Python lists or numpy arrays
@endcode

<b>Parameters:</b>
<table>
<tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>alpha</td><td>Vector</td><td>Initial probability vector (possibly non-normalized)</td></tr>
<tr><td>A</td><td>Matrix</td><td>Matrix parameter with negative eigenvalues</td></tr>
</table>

<b>Validation:</b> The constructor performs strict validation using BuTools CheckMERepresentation:
- Verifies \f$\alpha\f$ and \f$A\f$ have compatible dimensions
- Checks all eigenvalues of \f$A\f$ have negative real parts
- Ensures the dominant eigenvalue is real

@subsubsection me_factory_methods Factory Methods

<b>ME.fromExp(rate)</b> - Create ME from exponential distribution

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
me = ME.fromExp(rate)  % MATLAB
ME me = ME.fromExp(rate);  // Java
me = ME.from_exp(rate)  # Python
@endcode

<b>ME.fromErlang(k, rate)</b> - Create ME from Erlang-k distribution

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
me = ME.fromErlang(k, rate)  % MATLAB
ME me = ME.fromErlang(k, rate);  // Java
me = ME.from_erlang(k, rate)  # Python
@endcode

<b>ME.fromHyperExp(p, rates)</b> - Create ME from hyper-exponential distribution

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
me = ME.fromHyperExp(p, rates)  % MATLAB
ME me = ME.fromHyperExp(p, rates);  // Java
me = ME.from_hyper_exp(p, rates)  # Python
@endcode

<b>ME.fitMoments(moments)</b> - Fit ME to match given moments

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
me = ME.fitMoments([m1, m2, m3])  % MATLAB
ME me = ME.fitMoments(new double[]{m1, m2, m3});  // Java
me = ME.fit_moments([m1, m2, m3])  # Python
@endcode

<b>Description:</b> Uses BuTools MEFromMoments algorithm to fit an ME distribution
matching the specified factorial moments.

@subsection me_methods Instance Methods

<b>getMean()</b> - Compute mean of the distribution

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
mean = me.getMean()  % MATLAB
double mean = me.getMean();  // Java
mean = me.get_mean()  # Python
@endcode

<b>getVar()</b> - Compute variance of the distribution

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
var = me.getVar()  % MATLAB
double var = me.getVar();  // Java
var = me.get_var()  # Python
@endcode

<b>getSCV()</b> - Compute squared coefficient of variation

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
scv = me.getSCV()  % MATLAB
double scv = me.getSCV();  // Java
scv = me.get_scv()  # Python
@endcode

<b>evalCDF(t)</b> - Evaluate cumulative distribution function at time t

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
F = me.evalCDF(t)  % MATLAB
double F = me.evalCDF(t);  // Java
F = me.eval_cdf(t)  # Python
@endcode

<b>getAlpha()</b> - Get initial vector

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
alpha = me.getAlpha()  % MATLAB - returns Matrix
Matrix alpha = me.getAlpha();  // Java
alpha = me.get_alpha()  # Python - returns numpy array
@endcode

<b>getA()</b> - Get matrix parameter

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
A = me.getA()  % MATLAB - returns Matrix
Matrix A = me.getA();  // Java
A = me.get_a()  # Python - returns numpy array
@endcode

<b>getNumberOfPhases()</b> - Get number of phases

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
n = me.getNumberOfPhases()  % MATLAB
int n = me.getNumberOfPhases();  // Java
n = me.get_number_of_phases()  # Python
@endcode

@subsection me_sampling Sampling

<b>me_sample(ME, n)</b> - Generate random samples

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
samples = me_sample(ME, n)  % MATLAB
double[] samples = me_sample(alpha, A, n, random);  // Java (API function)
samples = me.sample(n)  # Python
@endcode

<b>Description:</b> Generates random samples using inverse CDF interpolation:
1. Precomputes CDF on a grid of 1000 points from 0 to mean + 10σ
2. For each sample, generates u ~ Uniform(0,1)
3. Uses binary search + linear interpolation to find \f$F^{-1}(u)\f$

This method ensures accurate sampling even for non-normalized \f$\alpha\f$ vectors.

@subsection me_solver_support Solver Support

<b>MAM Solver:</b> Full support via Markovian base class. ME distributions are automatically
converted to process representation {D0, D1} and analyzed using matrix analytic methods.

<b>LDES Solver:</b> Full support via me_sample function with inverse CDF interpolation.

<b>Other Solvers:</b> ME distributions can be used anywhere PH distributions are supported,
as they provide compatible process representations.

---

@section rap_overview Rational Arrival Process (RAP)

The Rational Arrival Process is a generalization of the Markovian Arrival Process (MAP).
It is parameterized by two matrices \f$H_0\f$ and \f$H_1\f$, where \f$H_0 + H_1\f$ forms
a generator matrix.

<b>Mathematical Definition:</b>

RAP is defined by:
- \f$H_0\f$: Transition rate matrix for hidden (non-arrival) events
- \f$H_1\f$: Transition rate matrix for visible (arrival) events
- \f$Q = H_0 + H_1\f$ must be a valid generator matrix (rows sum to 0)

<b>Properties:</b>
- All eigenvalues of \f$H_0\f$ must have negative real parts
- The dominant eigenvalue of \f$H_0\f$ must be real
- The marginal inter-arrival time distribution is an ME distribution
- Can model correlated arrivals with lag-k autocorrelations

<b>Process Representation:</b>

RAP distributions are represented as:
- \f$D_0 = H_0\f$
- \f$D_1 = H_1\f$

This is identical to MAP representation, enabling use of all MAP-based algorithms.

@subsection rap_constructors Constructors

@subsubsection rap_constructor_basic Basic Constructor

<b>MATLAB:</b>
@code
rap = RAP(H0, H1)
@endcode

<b>Java:</b>
@code
RAP rap = new RAP(H0, H1);
@endcode

<b>Python:</b>
@code
rap = RAP(H0, H1)  # H0 and H1 can be Python lists or numpy arrays
@endcode

<b>Parameters:</b>
<table>
<tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>H0</td><td>Matrix</td><td>Hidden transition rate matrix</td></tr>
<tr><td>H1</td><td>Matrix</td><td>Visible transition rate matrix (arrivals)</td></tr>
</table>

<b>Validation:</b> The constructor performs strict validation using BuTools CheckRAPRepresentation:
- Verifies \f$H_0\f$ and \f$H_1\f$ are square and compatible
- Checks \f$H_0 + H_1\f$ is a valid generator (row sums = 0)
- Verifies all eigenvalues of \f$H_0\f$ have negative real parts
- Ensures the dominant eigenvalue of \f$H_0\f$ is real

@subsubsection rap_factory_methods Factory Methods

<b>RAP.fromPoisson(rate)</b> - Create RAP from Poisson process

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
rap = RAP.fromPoisson(rate)  % MATLAB
RAP rap = RAP.fromPoisson(rate);  // Java
rap = RAP.from_poisson(rate)  # Python
@endcode

<b>Description:</b> Creates a 1-phase RAP equivalent to a Poisson process with the given rate.

<b>RAP.fromErlang(k, rate)</b> - Create RAP from Erlang renewal process

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
rap = RAP.fromErlang(k, rate)  % MATLAB
RAP rap = RAP.fromErlang(k, rate);  // Java
rap = RAP.from_erlang(k, rate)  # Python
@endcode

<b>Description:</b> Creates a k-phase RAP representing an Erlang-k renewal process.

<b>RAP.fromMAP(map)</b> - Create RAP from MAP

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
rap = RAP.fromMAP(map)  % MATLAB
RAP rap = RAP.fromMAP(map);  // Java
rap = RAP.from_map(map)  # Python
@endcode

<b>Description:</b> Converts a MAP to a RAP. Every MAP is a valid RAP, so this is a
direct conversion that preserves all properties.

@subsection rap_methods Instance Methods

<b>getMean()</b> - Compute mean inter-arrival time

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
mean = rap.getMean()  % MATLAB
double mean = rap.getMean();  // Java
mean = rap.get_mean()  # Python
@endcode

<b>getVar()</b> - Compute variance of inter-arrival time

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
var = rap.getVar()  % MATLAB
double var = rap.getVar();  // Java
var = rap.get_var()  # Python
@endcode

<b>getSCV()</b> - Compute squared coefficient of variation

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
scv = rap.getSCV()  % MATLAB
double scv = rap.getSCV();  // Java
scv = rap.get_scv()  # Python
@endcode

<b>getRate()</b> - Compute arrival rate (1/mean)

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
rate = rap.getRate()  % MATLAB
double rate = rap.getRate();  // Java
rate = rap.get_rate()  # Python
@endcode

<b>getH0()</b> - Get hidden transition matrix

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
H0 = rap.getH0()  % MATLAB - returns Matrix
Matrix H0 = rap.getH0();  // Java
H0 = rap.get_h0()  # Python - returns numpy array
@endcode

<b>getH1()</b> - Get visible transition matrix

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
H1 = rap.getH1()  % MATLAB - returns Matrix
Matrix H1 = rap.getH1();  // Java
H1 = rap.get_h1()  # Python - returns numpy array
@endcode

<b>getNumberOfPhases()</b> - Get number of phases

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
n = rap.getNumberOfPhases()  % MATLAB
int n = rap.getNumberOfPhases();  // Java
n = rap.get_number_of_phases()  # Python
@endcode

@subsection rap_sampling Sampling

<b>rap_sample(RAP, n)</b> - Generate correlated arrival samples

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
samples = rap_sample(RAP, n)  % MATLAB
samples = rap.sample(n)  # Python
@endcode

<b>Description:</b> Generates correlated inter-arrival time samples preserving the
lag-k autocorrelations of the RAP. Uses the marginal ME distribution for inverse CDF
interpolation.

@subsection rap_solver_support Solver Support

<b>MAM Solver:</b> Full support via Markovian base class. RAP processes are analyzed using
matrix analytic methods compatible with MAP algorithms.

<b>LDES Solver:</b> Full support via rap_sample function with correlated sampling.

<b>Other Solvers:</b> RAP can be used anywhere MAP is supported, as they provide identical
process representations.

---

@section me_rap_validation Validation Functions

@subsection check_me_rep CheckMERepresentation

<b>checkMERepresentation(alpha, A)</b> - Validate ME representation

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
valid = CheckMERepresentation(alpha, A)  % MATLAB
boolean valid = checkMERepresentation(alpha, A);  // Java
@endcode

<b>Returns:</b> true if (alpha, A) forms a valid ME representation, false otherwise.

<b>Validation Criteria:</b>
- \f$\alpha\f$ and \f$A\f$ have compatible dimensions
- \f$A\f$ is square
- All eigenvalues of \f$A\f$ have negative real parts
- The dominant eigenvalue (closest to 0) is real
- Numerical stability checks

@subsection check_rap_rep CheckRAPRepresentation

<b>checkRAPRepresentation(H0, H1)</b> - Validate RAP representation

<b>Syntax:</b>
@code
valid = CheckRAPRepresentation(H0, H1)  % MATLAB
boolean valid = checkRAPRepresentation(H0, H1);  // Java
@endcode

<b>Returns:</b> true if (H0, H1) forms a valid RAP representation, false otherwise.

<b>Validation Criteria:</b>
- \f$H_0\f$ and \f$H_1\f$ are square matrices of the same size
- \f$H_0 + H_1\f$ is a valid generator matrix (row sums = 0)
- All eigenvalues of \f$H_0\f$ have negative real parts
- The dominant eigenvalue of \f$H_0\f$ is real
- Numerical stability checks

---

@section me_rap_examples Usage in Queueing Models

@subsection example_map_me MAP/ME/1 Queue

An open queueing system with MAP arrivals and ME service times:

<b>MATLAB:</b>
@code
model = Network('MAP/ME/1');
source = Source(model, 'Source');
queue = Queue(model, 'Queue', SchedStrategy.FCFS);
sink = Sink(model, 'Sink');
jobClass = OpenClass(model, 'Class1', 0);

% MAP arrivals
D0 = [-2, 1; 0.5, -1.5];
D1 = [0.5, 0.5; 0.5, 0.5];
source.setArrival(jobClass, MAP(D0, D1));

% ME service (Erlang-2)
queue.setService(jobClass, ME.fromErlang(2, 2.0));

% Solve with MAM
solver = SolverMAM(model);
avgTable = solver.getAvgTable();
@endcode

<b>Java:</b>
@code
Network model = new Network("MAP/ME/1");
Source source = new Source(model, "Source");
Queue queue = new Queue(model, "Queue", SchedStrategy.FCFS);
Sink sink = new Sink(model, "Sink");
OpenClass jobClass = new OpenClass(model, "Class1", 0);

// MAP arrivals
Matrix D0 = new Matrix(new double[][]{{-2, 1}, {0.5, -1.5}});
Matrix D1 = new Matrix(new double[][]{{0.5, 0.5}, {0.5, 0.5}});
source.setArrival(jobClass, new MAP(D0, D1));

// ME service (Erlang-2)
queue.setService(jobClass, ME.fromErlang(2, 2.0));

// Solve with MAM
SolverMAM solver = new SolverMAM(model);
NetworkAvgTable avgTable = solver.getAvgTable();
@endcode

<b>Python:</b>
@code
model = Network("MAP/ME/1")
source = Source(model, "Source")
queue = Queue(model, "Queue", SchedStrategy.FCFS)
sink = Sink(model, "Sink")
job_class = OpenClass(model, "Class1", 0)

# MAP arrivals (using Python lists)
D0 = [[-2, 1], [0.5, -1.5]]
D1 = [[0.5, 0.5], [0.5, 0.5]]
source.set_arrival(job_class, MAP(D0, D1))

# ME service (Erlang-2)
queue.set_service(job_class, ME.from_erlang(2, 2.0))

# Solve with MAM
solver = SolverMAM(model)
avg_table = solver.get_avg_table()
@endcode

@subsection example_rap_ph RAP/PH/1 Queue

An open queueing system with RAP arrivals and PH service times:

<b>MATLAB:</b>
@code
model = Network('RAP/PH/1');
source = Source(model, 'Source');
queue = Queue(model, 'Queue', SchedStrategy.FCFS);
jobClass = OpenClass(model, 'Class1', 0);

% RAP arrivals (Erlang-2)
source.setArrival(jobClass, RAP.fromErlang(2, 1.0));

% PH service
alpha = [1, 0];
A = [-2, 2; 0, -2];
queue.setService(jobClass, PH(alpha, A));
@endcode

---

